A **class** bundles data with the **member functions** that work on it, and **access specifiers** (`public`, `private`) say who may touch what. Which line fails to compile, and why?

```cpp
class Counter {
public:
    void tick() { ++n_; }
    int value() { return n_; }
private:
    int n_ = 0;
};
int main() {
    Counter c;
    c.tick();
    return c.n_;   // line 11
}
```

`Probe`'s constructor prints `+name ` and its destructor prints `-name `. What does `main` print in total?

```cpp
struct Probe {
    const char* name;
    Probe(const char* n) : name(n) { std::printf("+%s ", name); }
    ~Probe() { std::printf("-%s ", name); }
};
int main() {
    Probe a("A");
    {
        Probe b("B");
        Probe c("C");
    }
    std::printf("| ");
}
```

A **scope guard** is a small RAII object that does something at the start of a block and undoes it at the end. Write the missing constructor and destructor for `IrqLock`, which must keep interrupts off for the lifetime of the object. (An interrupt is a hardware signal that pauses the running code to run a handler. `disable_irq()` and `enable_irq()` exist.)

```cpp
class IrqLock {
public:
    IrqLock()  { /* ??? */ }
    ~IrqLock() { /* ??? */ }
    IrqLock(const IrqLock&) = delete;
    IrqLock& operator=(const IrqLock&) = delete;
};
```

Using the `IrqLock` guard (constructor disables interrupts, destructor enables them), what does this print?

```cpp
static int irq_off = 0;
void disable_irq() { irq_off = 1; }
void enable_irq()  { irq_off = 0; }
int take(int x) {
    IrqLock lock;
    if (x < 0) return -1;
    return x * 2;
}
int main() {
    int a = take(-5), b = take(4);
    std::printf("%d %d irq_off=%d\n", a, b, irq_off);
}
```

This class owns a heap buffer. What goes wrong at runtime, and what is the general rule that it breaks?

```cpp
class Buf {
public:
    Buf(int n) : p_(new char[n]) {}
    ~Buf() { delete[] p_; }
private:
    char* p_;
};
int main() {
    Buf a(16);
    Buf b = a;   // copy
}
```

What does this program print?

```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <utility>
struct T {
    T() = default;
    T(const T&)     { std::puts("copy"); }
    T(T&&) noexcept { std::puts("move"); }
};
int main() {
    T a;
    T b = a;
    T c = std::move(a);
}
