`Buffer` owns a heap array. Fill in the move constructor body so the new object takes the old object's array and the old object is left safe to destroy.

```cpp
class Buffer {
public:
    Buffer(std::size_t n) : p_(new char[n]), n_(n) {}
    ~Buffer() { delete[] p_; }
    Buffer(Buffer&& o) noexcept : p_(o.p_), n_(o.n_) {
        /* ??? */
    }
private:
    char* p_;
    std::size_t n_;
};
```

This move assignment compiles and runs, but it has a bug. What is it?

```cpp
Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& o) noexcept {
    p_ = o.p_;
    n_ = o.n_;
    o.p_ = nullptr;
    o.n_ = 0;
    return *this;
}
```
(`Buffer` owns `char* p_` allocated with `new[]`, and its destructor calls `delete[] p_`.)

After running, what does this program print? Assume `Buffer` has the move constructor and `size()` from the previous cards (a moved-from object has `n_ == 0`).

```cpp
int main() {
    Buffer a(64);
    Buffer b(std::move(a));
    std::printf("%zu %zu\n", a.size(), b.size());
}
```

**Return value optimization (RVO)** lets the compiler build a returned object directly in the caller's variable. With a C++17 or later compiler, what does this print?

```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <utility>
struct W {
    W()           { std::puts("make"); }
    W(const W&)     { std::puts("copy"); }
    W(W&&) noexcept { std::puts("move"); }
};
W good() { return W(); }
W bad()  { W w; return std::move(w); }
int main() {
    W a = good();
    std::puts("--");
    W b = bad();
}
```

A **non-type template parameter** is a template parameter that is a value, such as the capacity `N` below, not a type. Fill in the full-buffer condition so `push` refuses to overwrite unread data.

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
template <typename T, std::size_t N>
class RingBuffer {
    T data_[N];
    std::size_t head_ = 0, count_ = 0;
public:
    bool push(const T& v) {
        if (/* ??? */) return false;
        data_[(head_ + count_) % N] = v;
        ++count_;
        return true;
    }
};
```

